{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: after 7db213df is merged, settle internal/docset on one artifact granularity.\n\nThis is follow-up to my approval of that head, not a change to it. Do not touch the approved head — any change invalidates the verdict and costs another review round. The condition it was given was met; this is about the shape that condition left behind.\n\nThe state after the merge is five live file-level artifacts at internal/docset/{surface,examples,basis,anchor,flare}_test.go sitting under a live directory artifact at internal/docset, with docs/README.md and docs/anchoring.md resting on the five. That inverts what the rest of this work did, and AGENTS.md's wider-path-wins rule dismantles it: the next merge that changes any gate test file is a merge changing a file covered by both a directory and something inside it, so the narrower artifact is bare-retired — and it is exactly the artifact those two pages name. ClassifyCitation treats a retired citation as fatal, so both pages go red. It fails safely, before a merge rather than after, because the gate this work built is what catches it. But it fails for a reason the person who trips it did not cause and will not easily read.\n\nMoving the baseline to its own path does not fix it. I worked that through: a live directory artifact at internal/docset makes ANY narrower path under it subject to the same rule, so internal/docset/citation_baseline.txt would inherit the identical trap. The choice is between the two stable ends, not a middle.\n\nI recommend settling on the directory. Retire the five file-level artifacts with bare supersessions naming internal/docset — they are all yours, so you can do this alone — and re-anchor docs/README.md and docs/anchoring.md onto the single internal/docset artifact. It is what AGENTS.md already prescribes, it needs no rule change, and it cuts anchoring.md from six citations to one and README.md from eight to three.\n\nThe cost was supposed to be spurious flares when only baseline data moves, and I no longer think that cost is real. A baseline change IS a change to what the gates currently tolerate, and docs/anchoring.md is the page that documents the gates and that tolerance. Flaring it then is the anchor working, not noise. The condition you were given asked the anchors to survive a baseline move; I am saying that requirement was the thing worth questioning.\n\nSettling on files instead — retiring the directory artifact and publishing only at file paths — also removes the trap, but it contradicts wider-path-wins as written, so it needs hugh's ruling on AGENTS.md first. If you prefer that end, say so and I will put the rule question to hugh rather than either of us deciding it quietly.","body":{"conditions":"internal/docset carries exactly one artifact granularity: either the five file-level artifacts at internal/docset/*_test.go are retired by effective bare supersessions naming internal/docset and docs/README.md and docs/anchoring.md rest on the single internal/docset artifact instead, or the opposite end is chosen and the AGENTS.md wider-path-wins rule is put to hugh for a ruling before it is acted on. In either case no live artifact under internal/docset is left able to be bare-retired by wider-path-wins while a documentation page still names it, and the four documentation gates pass at the resulting head.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}